.TH CRUNCH-AS 1 "November 2026" "crunch-as 0.9.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
crunch-as \- assemble and link x86-64 Linux ELF programs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B crunch-as
[\fB\-c\fR] [\fB\-nostdlib\fR] [\fB\-I\fR \fIdir\fR]
[\fB\-o\fR \fIoutput\fR] [\fIinput.s\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B crunch-as
assembles x86-64 AT&T syntax into ELF64 relocatable objects.
By default, it assembles each input separately and invokes the
.B crunch-ld
executable located beside the assembler to produce an executable.
The default executable name is
.BR a.out .
The assembler supports a subset of GNU assembler syntax.
.PP
An input named
.B \-
reads standard input; if no input is supplied, standard input is used.
The assembler does not run a C preprocessor.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c
Assemble without linking. Without
.BR \-o ,
write one object per input in the current directory, replacing a trailing
.B .s
with
.BR .o ,
or appending
.B .o
otherwise. Standard input produces
.BR stdin.o .
If multiple default object paths refer to the same file, the command is
rejected before writing any output. Dangling output symlinks are also
rejected with multiple inputs.
Objects are written to temporary files in the destination directory and
replaced individually after successful writes; a multi-object invocation can
therefore update some outputs even if another output fails. Existing object destinations
must be regular files with no other hard links; symbolic links are rejected.
Existing ownership and ordinary permission bits are retained, but other
metadata such as ACLs and extended attributes may not be retained.
The destination directory must permit temporary file creation and replacement.
When built with Crunch, object output also requires a mounted
.BR /proc .
With
.BR \-o ,
all inputs are assembled together into one object.
.TP
.BI \-o " output"
Write the executable or object to
.IR output .
An output that refers to a command-line input or an opened
.B .include
file, including through a symbolic or hard link, is rejected.
.TP
.BI \-I " dir"
Add a directory to the search path for assembler
.B .include
directives. Relative names are searched first in the current working
directory, then in the specified directories in order.
.B \-Idir
is also accepted.
.TP
.B \-nostdlib
Pass this option to
.B crunch-ld
to disable automatic Crunch runtime selection.
For other linker options, assemble with
.B \-c
and invoke
.B crunch-ld
separately.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print usage information and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Directory for temporary objects when linking or assembling multiple inputs
with
.B \-c
without
.BR \-o .
Defaults to
.B /tmp
when unset or empty.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success; a nonzero status indicates an assembler or linker
failure.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
crunch-as \-nostdlib \-o program program.s
crunch-as \-c \-o program.o program.s
crunch-ld \-nostdlib \-o program program.o
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR crunch-ld (1)
.PP
The installed documentation includes
.B docs/assembler.md
for supported syntax and limitations.
